(3200709, 283627, 69700, '<td valign="top">This is beautiful photo, everything is perfect, and the photographer definitely deserved to win the blue ribbon.   <br/> <br/>I do have some questions though, why would ANYONE give this photo a vote of less than than a 6?  I understand that the voting is subjective, but there is no way anyone would think this is a below average photo, yet 39 or about 15% of the voters thought it was below average.  It would appear that some people give those photographs who they view as competition, a low score to improve their ranking in the contest.  I am new here, but is this common?<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-01-09 14:44:37.</i></td>', 'This is beautiful photo, everything is perfect, and the photographer definitely deserved to win the blue ribbon.    I do have some questions though, why would ANYONE give this photo a vote of less than than a 6?  I understand that the voting is subjective, but there is no way anyone would think this is a below average photo, yet 39 or about 15% of the voters thought it was below average.  It would appear that some people give those photographs who they view as competition, a low score to improve their ranking in the contest.  I am new here, but is this common?', '2007-01-09 14:39:30.000000', '2007-01-09 14:44:37.000000', 0),
(3203581, 283627, 56954, '<td valign="top">Wow, I feel like this person is very powerful and I am cowering under his gaze. An amazing and evocative capture. Deserves a 20.</td>', 'Wow, I feel like this person is very powerful and I am cowering under his gaze. An amazing and evocative capture. Deserves a 20.', '2007-01-10 01:36:26.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(3236096, 285675, 31637, '<td valign="top">Oh that air you must be breathing.... envy you for this one !</td>', 'Oh that air you must be breathing.... envy you for this one !', '2007-01-15 17:39:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(3308297, 284708, 73240, '<td valign="top">I had the privledge of assisting for Martin during a magazine shoot a few years ago.<br/>He''s been using this method of portraiture for some time now.<br/> <br/>I think you''ve done an excellent job at emulating this, and please allow me to criticize the criticism made about this being "not warm enough".<br/>True, some of his are warm (filtered light).<br/> <br/>Martin uses 2 banks of a non-traditional photo lighting source which are perfectly balanced for daylight and lend more of a cool tone.<br/> <br/>great emulation...</td>', 'I had the privledge of assisting for Martin during a magazine shoot a few years ago.He''s been using this method of portraiture for some time now. I think you''ve done an excellent job at emulating this, and please allow me to criticize the criticism made about this being "not warm enough".True, some of his are warm (filtered light). Martin uses 2 banks of a non-traditional photo lighting source which are perfectly balanced for daylight and lend more of a cool tone. great emulation...', '2007-01-31 13:19:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(3337095, 284864, 48890, '<td valign="top">For the sake of anyone opening this image, I must say that I''ve since learned that Igor Posner''s photographs are, probably, inspired by <a href="//www.agencevu.com/fr/photographes/default.asp?Photographes=1" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Michael Ackerman</a>.</td>', 'For the sake of anyone opening this image, I must say that I''ve since learned that Igor Posner''s photographs are, probably, inspired by Michael Ackerman.', '2007-02-06 23:24:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(3431855, 283278, 68504, '<td valign="top">What I like is the busy foreground, then the unbusy skyscrapers -so smooth, linear, and then the wonderfully similarly toned sky with cloud.  Never seen any composition like it, though indeed have probably passed this on the highway, it is very vaguely familiar.</td>', 'What I like is the busy foreground, then the unbusy skyscrapers -so smooth, linear, and then the wonderfully similarly toned sky with cloud.  Never seen any composition like it, though indeed have probably passed this on the highway, it is very vaguely familiar.', '2007-03-05 19:58:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(3445996, 283627, 73884, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by lantaga:</b></div><hr/><i> This is beautiful photo, everything is perfect, and the photographer definitely deserved to win the blue ribbon.   <br/> <br/>I do have some questions though, why would ANYONE give this photo a vote of less than than a 6?  I understand that the voting is subjective, but there is no way anyone would think this is a below average photo, yet 39 or about 15% of the voters thought it was below average.  It would appear that some people give those photographs who they view as competition, a low score to improve their ranking in the contest.  I am new here, but is this common? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>This is a great photo but some low marks may reflect the voter''s view of how close it resembles Andrzej Dragan''s work (which are very dramatic and amazing considering that by trade he is a professor with a PhD in quantum physics). Btw, I did not vote in this challenge (I wasn''t a member yet) so it wasn''t me with the low marks, I would''ve given it a 10.</td>', 'Originally posted by lantaga: This is beautiful photo, everything is perfect, and the photographer definitely deserved to win the blue ribbon.    I do have some questions though, why would ANYONE give this photo a vote of less than than a 6?  I understand that the voting is subjective, but there is no way anyone would think this is a below average photo, yet 39 or about 15% of the voters thought it was below average.  It would appear that some people give those photographs who they view as competition, a low score to improve their ranking in the contest.  I am new here, but is this common?   This is a great photo but some low marks may reflect the voter''s view of how close it resembles Andrzej Dragan''s work (which are very dramatic and amazing considering that by trade he is a professor with a PhD in quantum physics). Btw, I did not vote in this challenge (I wasn''t a member yet) so it wasn''t me with the low marks, I would''ve given it a 10.', '2007-03-09 10:56:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(3905477, 285434, 63269, '<td valign="top">This is so subtle. The commenter below said that it looked like it was all about to disapear. I completely agree - when I look at this, I am reminded of a breezy afternoon in the forest. The clouds are drifting around, and the sunlight keeps appearing and disappearing in patches, and with it, the shadows. It''s like you captured that brief and silent moment when the sun shone for a moment, and the shadows came out. <br/> <br/>Brilliant shot.</td>', 'This is so subtle. The commenter below said that it looked like it was all about to disapear. I completely agree - when I look at this, I am reminded of a breezy afternoon in the forest. The clouds are drifting around, and the sunlight keeps appearing and disappearing in patches, and with it, the shadows. It''s like you captured that brief and silent moment when the sun shone for a moment, and the shadows came out.  Brilliant shot.', '2007-07-15 17:39:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(4055501, 284112, 27133, '<td valign="top">I have some just like this one...lots of fun.  I am off to check out the original now.</td>', 'I have some just like this one...lots of fun.  I am off to check out the original now.', '2007-09-04 03:27:57.000000', NULL, 0),
